  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Monday)\nIssued at 835 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe long term period will begin with zonal flow aloft as the TN\nValley sits between low pressure off the western Atlantic and an\namplifying ridge over the southern Plains. This will ultimately\nresult in tropical air advection ahead of an upper wave progged to\nmove across the central Plains and into the upper Midwest over the\nweekend, bringing increased moisture and above normal temperatures to\nthe area. There is some indication that a secondary low may form \nacross the Ozarks/mid MS River Valley that would enhance synoptic \nlift closer to home and would bring increasing rain chances on \nHalloween. For now we will stick with blended guidance, bringing low \nto medium (30-50%) chances for rain beginning Halloween afternoon and\nincreasing overnight, and continuing through Sunday. While severe \nweather does not look to be a threat with this system at this time, \nlow chances for thunderstorms will exist on Sunday. \n\nThis system will exit the area late Sunday and high pressure will\nmove into the area by the start of the next work week. This looks to\nbe short lived, as another and potentially more dynamic system sits\njust beyond the day 7 period and could bring additional rain and\nthunderstorms to the area early next week. Since this is beyond the\n7 day forecast period at this time, we will hold on for better\nforecast consistency but feel it will bear watching as it may impact\nElection Day.",
